import math

def predict_spin(n_d, delta_o, pairing):
    if 4 <= n_d <= 7 and delta_o > pairing:
        spin = "low"
        unpaired = {4: 2, 5: 1, 6: 0, 7: 1}[n_d]
    else:
        spin = "high"
        unpaired = n_d if n_d <= 5 else 10 - n_d
    return spin, unpaired, round(math.sqrt(unpaired * (unpaired + 2)), 2)

exercises.solution:
Going from high to low spin moves $k$ electrons from $e_g$ to $t_{2g}$ ($k$ = 1, 2, 2, 1 for d⁴–d⁷), changing the energy by $k(P - \Delta_o)$, so low spin is favoured exactly when $\Delta_o \gt P$. Then count unpaired electrons:

```python
import math

def predict_spin(n_d, delta_o, pairing):
    if 4 <= n_d <= 7 and delta_o > pairing:
        spin, unpaired = "low", {4: 2, 5: 1, 6: 0, 7: 1}[n_d]
    else:
        spin, unpaired = "high", (n_d if n_d <= 5 else 10 - n_d)
    return spin, unpaired, round(math.sqrt(unpaired * (unpaired + 2)), 2)
```

The three named complexes give ("high", 4, 4.9), ("low", 0, 0.0) and ("high", 5, 5.92). The measured moment of [Mn(H₂O)₆]²⁺, close to 5.9 μB, is one of the cleanest confirmations of the spin-only formula, because a high-spin d⁵ ion has no orbital contribution.
